<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  This file exists for the two keys that cannot be build settings.

	  Everything else in Bleat's Info.plist is generated: the target sets
	  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE and a handful of INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings,
	  which is the house style and means there is no plist to drift. But the
	  generated set covers a fixed list of keys, and neither CFBundleURLTypes
	  nor NSBonjourServices is on it — the second because it is an array, which
	  a build setting cannot express. Xcode merges the generated keys *into*
	  this file when both settings are present, so everything else, including
	  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription, lives in the project as an
	  INFOPLIST_KEY_.

	  The scheme string must match PairingPayload.urlScheme in BleatKit, which
	  is what `bleat pair` puts in the QR. There is no way to assert that from
	  a package test — this file belongs to the app target — so it is stated in
	  both places and in CLAUDE.md.

	  NSBonjourServices is not optional. Herdr runs on a Mac reached over SSH
	  at a `.local` name or a LAN address, both of which iOS gates behind the
	  local network permission; without this declaration NWBrowser is refused
	  before the permission is ever consulted, and LocalNetworkAccess — which
	  infers the permission from that browser — would conclude "blocked" and
	  never retry. The string must match LocalNetworkAccess.serviceType.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>dev.herdr.bleat.pairing</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>bleat</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_ssh._tcp</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
